    "durationMs": 498116,
    "finalText": "Implementation complete. The URL shortener passes every requirement, verified end-to-end:\n\n**What I built**\n- `src/main.rs` — axum 0.8.4 + sqlx (PostgreSQL) server: `POST /links` (201, generated or custom codes, URL returned unchanged), `409` on duplicate custom code, `GET /links/{code}`, `GET /r/{code}` (explicit 302 + Location, atomic `UPDATE ... clicks + 1 RETURNING url`), `GET /links` (ascending by id, `items` + `total`), and `404` JSON errors for unknown codes. A custom `ValidJson` extractor maps all JSON rejections (malformed body, missing/invalid fields, missing content type) to `422` with an `error` field. `DATABASE_URL` is read from the environment; nothing is hardcoded.\n- `migrations/0001_create_links.sql` — `links` table with unique code, applied via embedded `sqlx::migrate!` at startup.\n- `start.sh` — runs the prebuilt release binary (builds first if missing), which applies pending migrations then serves in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`.\n- `README.md` — startup instructions.\n\n**Verified by live HTTP tests**: all status codes (201/200/302/404/409/422), boundary code lengths (4 and 20 chars), invalid URLs (relative, ftp, empty host), malformed JSON, click incrementing exactly once per redirect, list ordering, and full data persistence across a server restart.\n\nTwo issues worth noting that I hit and fixed: rand 0.9's `ThreadRng` is `!Send` (breaking axum's Handler bound — switched to OS-seeded `StdRng`), and axum's `Redirect::temporary` is actually 307, so the redirect is built explicitly with `StatusCode::FOUND` + Location header.\n\nThe database was truncated and port 8000 left free, so the test suite starts from a clean slate with `sh start.sh`."
  }
